   222	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   223	    """Extend anchor-aligned grey rays, treat the resulting enclosed rectangles as containers, and tile-match each source sprite into its unique rectangle.
   224	
   225	    Input structure:
   226	    - The grid has a black background, one solid 3x3 grey anchor, several isolated grey marker pixels, and 2-3 small monochrome source sprites in other colors.
   227	    - Some grey markers share one anchor row or one anchor column; other grey markers are unaligned noise.
   228	    - The anchor, the valid marker rays, and the grid boundary jointly carve the non-grey-free space into a mix of open regions, of which exactly 2-3 are perfect rectangles.
   229	
   230	    Rule:
   231	    - Keep the 3x3 grey anchor and erase every original grey marker before drawing anything else.
   232	    - For each grey marker aligned with an anchor row or anchor column, draw a grey horizontal or vertical ray from that marker to the cell adjacent to the anchor; ignore and erase unaligned grey markers.
   233	    - On the ray-augmented grid, find every 4-connected non-grey region enclosed by grey and/or the grid boundary whose occupied cells form an exact rectangle.
   234	    - For each source sprite, test whether its cropped shape can tile one rectangle by pure translation with no rotation or reflection; use the unique exact sprite-to-rectangle matching.
   235	    - Erase the original sprite positions and fill each matched rectangle by repeating that sprite pattern from the rectangle's top-left corner.
   236	
   237	    Key insight:
   238	    - The destination containers are not explicit input objects: they emerge only after the aligned grey markers are extended into rays, and the final sprite placement is determined by geometric tiling compatibility rather than by color or source position.
